<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Verkhovna Rada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Supreme Council of Ukraine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Verkhovna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a>, or Supreme Council, is the parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, a single chamber of 450 deputies. It began as the Supreme Soviet of the Ukrainian SSR, and after the first competitive elections of 1990 it declared Ukraine's state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> that July. On 24 August 1991 it adopted the Act of Declaration of Independence of Ukraine, confirmed by referendum on 1 December 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/verkhovna-rada.jpg" alt="Verkhovna Rada" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EricLewan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verkhovna_Rada_(UK_logo,_2022).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> means council. The institution originated in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, when it was a council of boyars and of the higher clergy. In the 17th and 18th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a> used the term for the meetings where major decisions were made, and they elected new councils by popular vote. The Ukrainian People's Republic had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> between 17 March 1917 and 29 April 1918. The present name comes from the Soviet practice of calling national and republican parliaments Supreme Soviets, a term localised in the Ukrainian SSR as Verkhovna Rada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Supreme Soviet of the Ukrainian SSR was set up under the 1937 constitution, with its first elections on 26 June 1938 and its first session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> from 25 to 28 July 1938. Its first chairman was Mykhailo Burmystenko. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its Presidium was evacuated to Saratov, and it returned to Kyiv in 1944.</p><p>The first partially free elections were held on 4 March 1990. The Communist Party remained in control, but a Democratic Bloc that included the People's Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> (Rukh) was formed. The new parliament issued the Declaration of State Sovereignty on 16 July 1990 and declared independence on 24 August 1991, under its chairman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a>. A national referendum overwhelmingly supported the act on 1 December 1991. The Constitution of Ukraine was adopted on 28 June 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Verkhovna Rada is the sole body of legislative power. It has 450 deputies, elected by equal and direct universal suffrage by secret ballot, and it elects a chairman and two deputy chairmen from among its members. It adopts laws, amends the constitution, approves the state budget, appoints the prime minister, ratifies treaties and declares war and peace. Bills are usually considered in three readings, and the president must sign a law before it is promulgated. Electoral systems have varied: all seats were filled in single-member districts in 1990 and 1994, half in 1998 and 2002, and by party lists alone in 2006 and 2007.</p><p>The parliament meets in a neo-classical building on Mykhaila Hrushevsky Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, designed by Volodymyr Zabolotny after a competition in 1936. It was built from 1936 to 1938, destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and rebuilt from 1945 to 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Verkhovna Rada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The words followed only on 6 March 2003, when the <b>Verkhovna Rada</b> passed the Law on the State Anthem proposed by President Leonid Kuchma, by 334 votes of 450: the first stanza and refrain of Chubynskyi's poem, with the first line slightly changed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
